780 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 482 0 R
/E (a\) Standard form factor. The most commonly used cases are ATX and micro-ATX. The advantage of this type of case is that it can accommodate the most common motherboard form factors, such as ATX and micro-ATX. Micro-ATX cases have the advantage of taking up less space; however, the power supply they use, since it is not ATX-standard, may be difficult to replace.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
781 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 482 0 R
/E (b\) Smaller form factor. Mini-ITX cases. These usually support Mini-ITX motherboards, which are very common when a compact form factor is desired. This type of case typically includes a low-power power supply \(around 150 watts\). Some desktop models can be mounted on the back of a monitor, freeing up workspace. There are even smaller cases, such as Pico-ITX cases, a form factor commonly found in embedded systems.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
