924 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 495 0 R
/E (The BIOS is a program \(stored in a Flash EEPROM or read/write ROM memory\) whose data is stored in a CMOS memory. This memory is a type of RAM characterized by very low power consumption and is powered by a battery.)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
925 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 495 0 R
/E (This battery, typically a 3.3 V CR-2032, is located on the motherboard and usually lasts for several years. When it becomes depleted, the system often displays messages such as "CMOS Checksum Invalid" and loses its time and date settings.)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
