941 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 497 0 R
/E (If this is not possible \(for example, because the BIOS menu is protected with a password\), there are two alternative methods:)
/Pg 52 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
942 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2115 0 R
/E (Use the Clear CMOS or CLRCMOS jumper and then power on the computer. The effect is the same as selecting the corresponding option in the BIOS menu. \(Keep in mind that some motherboards do not include this jumper.\))
/Pg 52 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
943 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2116 0 R
/E (Remove the BIOS battery for approximately 10 seconds and then reinstall it. Without power, the BIOS CMOS memory loses its stored information and restores the default settings. \(If the BIOS battery must be removed, use a plastic object such as the cap of a pen. Never use a screwdriver or any metallic object.\))
